# The rock cube's material (T0157.4).
#
# Written by hand from claude_documentation/documentation/11-material-format.md,
# not saved out of the engine. Everything absent keeps its default, which is what
# makes a material this short a complete one.
#
# ## UV-mapped, not triplanar, and that was a deliberate change
#
# This started triplanar (T0141.8) because that is what T0156 built and a cube
# with no UVs is the sharpest demonstration of it. It is the wrong choice for a
# **rotating** object, and looking at it made that obvious: triplanar projects
# from *world* space, so the rock pattern is anchored to the world and the cube
# turns through it. The relief swims across the faces instead of travelling with
# them. That is inherent to the technique, not a defect — it is why triplanar
# belongs to terrain and static geometry, and why the mesh now carries a 0..1 UV
# island per face.
#
# The capability itself is unchanged and still proven by
# `tests/gpu/triplanar_test.cpp` and `tests/gpu/triplanar_parallax_test.cpp`.
#
# ## What that bought, beyond the swimming
#
# **The normal map.** A triplanar material ignores `normalTexture` outright
# (T0141.8), so the previous version of this file could not name one and every
# bit of surface shading came from the flat face normal. In UV space it applies,
# and it is the reason the rock now reads as a surface rather than a picture of
# one.
#
# One absence remains deliberate: **metallic** and **roughness** are left at 1,
# which multiplies into the ORM texture's blue and green channels. The rock set's
# blue is zero (test_assets/README.md), so the surface is a dielectric because
# the *texture* says so rather than because this file asserts it.
version: 2
material:
  baseColourTexture: 1570000000000010
  # Occlusion in red, roughness in green, metallic in blue — glTF's ORM packing,
  # so one texture fills two slots and that is not a mistake.
  metallicRoughnessTexture: 1570000000000011
  occlusionTexture: 1570000000000011
  # **OpenGL convention** (green is +Y), which is what glTF specifies and what
  # `test_assets` packs. The DirectX map has green inverted and the result is not
  # an obvious failure — it is lighting that looks subtly wrong, as though lit
  # from the opposite vertical direction.
  normalTexture: 1570000000000013
  # Eased off 1.0. At full strength this rock map perturbs a large share of an
  # already-dim face past N·L = 0, and with no ambient to fall back on those
  # texels clamp to solid black rather than going dark — detail turning into
  # a hole. 0.8 keeps the relief and stops the cliff.
  normalScale: 0.8
  heightTexture: 1570000000000012
  # Depth in UV0 units now, not projection units — one UV tile is one face, so
  # this is 4% of a 2-metre face, about 8 cm of apparent depth.
  #
  # Shallower than the 0.05 the triplanar version used, and for a reason worth
  # keeping: POM displaces **inward only** — the polygon face is the ceiling of
  # the relief and nothing rises above it — so deep relief behind a razor-flat
  # silhouette reads as a window into the surface rather than as carved rock.
  # Less depth, less window. Real protrusion needs geometry, which is T0146.7's
  # tessellation and explicitly not this.
  heightScale: 0.04
  # **The sample's own shader module** (T0158.1, T0159) — game content,
  # compiled into the surface pipeline and overriding two hooks with state
  # kept between them. It marches the same height map with a reference plane
  # at 0.5, so half the range reads as raised instead of the whole field
  # hanging beneath the face, and marches a second, shorter ray toward the
  # dominant light for self-shadowing. The engine is unchanged; this is what
  # D26, D28 and T0159's opened contract exist to make expressible.
  shader: 1570000000000020
  # **The module's own parameter** (T0160), and the line this whole file
  # existed without. `rock_pom.slang` declares `referencePlane` in its
  # `HpMaterialParams` block; until T0160 it was a compile-time literal,
  # because a game shader's own knobs had nowhere to live. Changing this number
  # rebuilds no pipeline and invalidates no cook — it is written into a
  # constant buffer per draw, exactly as `heightScale` above is.
  #
  # 0.5 puts half the height range above the polygon plane. 1.0 is the engine's
  # own behaviour, everything sunken.
  params:
    - name: referencePlane
      value: 0.5
